       User namespaces can be nested; that is, each user namespace —except the
       initial  ("root")  namespace— has a parent user namespace, and can have
       zero or more child user namespaces.  The parent user namespace  is  the
       user  namespace  of  the  process that creates the user namespace via a
       call to uunnsshhaarree(2) or cclloonnee(2) with the CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWUUSSEERR flag.

       The kernel imposes (since Linux 3.11) a limit of 32  nested  levels  of
       user namespaces.  Calls to uunnsshhaarree(2) or cclloonnee(2) that would cause this
       limit to be exceeded fail with the error EEUUSSEERRSS.

       Each process is a member of exactly one user namespace.  A process cre‐
       ated via ffoorrkk(2) or cclloonnee(2) without the CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWUUSSEERR flag is a member
       of  the  same  user namespace as its parent.  A single‐threaded process
       can join another user namespace with sseettnnss(2) if it has the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADD‐‐
       MMIINN in that namespace; upon doing so, it gains a full set of  capabili‐
       ties in that namespace.

       A  call to cclloonnee(2) or uunnsshhaarree(2) with the CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWUUSSEERR flag makes the
       new child process (for cclloonnee(2)) or the caller (for uunnsshhaarree(2)) a  mem‐
       ber of the new user namespace created by the call.

       The  NNSS__GGEETT__PPAARREENNTT  iiooccttll(2)  operation  can  be  used  to discover the
       parental relationship between user namespaces; see iiooccttll__nnssffss(2).

